A||||29||Mike Murphy||Fiero RIP 1984-1988  369,167 Produced||||3||
Z||000000||Mike Murphy||11-21-2001||10:29 PM||||It has occured to me that several people still don't have a clue as to why GM stopped building the Fiero and are distorting the facts unknowingly for those who seek the truth.<P>Few out there can say they have met or conversed with Hulki Aldikacti, Ed Benson, Bill Hoglund, Bill Lane, Bruce McDonald, John Middlebrook, or John Sawruck.  With the exception of Mr. Aldikacti I have done both on a formal and informal basis mostly about the thing we all loved the most,the wholesale automotive business.  Unless you worked on a project and helped bring a car to market that obtained some relative success you have no real knowlege of what goes on especially with a car so unique like the Fiero.<P>This car broke new ground for Pontiac and GM in it's design and manufacturing but it was not profitable in the end so reluctantly Pontiac pulled the plug.<P>Don't believe me? Think the Vette brass got upset and put the political heat on the Corporation to do away with the Fiero?  How about the Grand National? The GMC Cyclone and Typhoon? Don't forget the 89 Trans Am Pace Car!<P>If this is your thinking then the grey matter between your ears is brown!  If it hasn't quite changed colors then maybe you can understand Economics 101.  I will review in simple terms.  <P>Pontiac did sell the Corporation PPG Committee on the Fiero as a 50 MPG commuter car projecting sales of 100,000 units annualy.  This was with a 300 million dollar investment bringing it to market back in the late 70's, early 80's when GM was writing red ink for the first time in it's history.  In order to break even on the investment they needed to produce 70,000 units a year and an additional 5 to 10 thousand units to make a profit.  Not just one year either to recoup the investment.  The car was built 5 model years so in 5 years they needed to produce & sell 350,000 Fieros right?  <P>Here is the breakdown of total Fiero sales.<P>     84      85      86      87      88<BR>  136,840  76,371  83,974  46,581  26,401<P>1984 was the most succesfull year but even it is misleading as it was an extended build over 18 instead of the normal 12 month build due to design changes and mods for the V6 in 85.  The total 5 year run was 369,167 which is only slightly less than 20,000 units above the magic breakeven of 350,000 cars.  Couple that with a suddenly crowded 2 seat market in the low to mid price range and several recalls, engine reliability problems with the 2.5 and escalating insurance costs the outlook for improved sales was not favorable. The sales from 86 to 88 slid 68.50% which read the Fiero was headed for the obituaries.<P>Now lets look at the GMC Cyclone and Typhoon that some say embarassed the Corvette and lead to a very early demise.<P>Those 2 trucks according to GMC were the fastest production trucks ever with the turbo 4.3 V6 built from 91 to 93 based on the Sonoma/Jimmy platform but were not built in house as the engine and appearance items were done on a pass through basis to an outside company called, Production Automotive Services, Inc. (PAS) which also did the 89 Trans AM Pace Car with the stockpiled 3.8 Grand National engine.  Incidently the Grand National did not continue after GM phased out the rear drive Regal platform as no front drive transaxle could hold up to the brutal punishment.<P>The Cyclone & Typhoon were a great excercise in technology and an attempt for GMC to set itself apart from Chevrolet as an upgrade in truck alternatives.  Surprisingly Chevrolet even supplied the transmission and shifter from the Corvette as GM had no durable alternative.<P>Why did the pair leave the GM stable so quickly? Easy, poor sales and PAS soon went out of business.  Let's take a look at a sales comparison of the Cyclone & Typhoon to total production of the Sonoma & Jimmy.<P>           91       92      93<BR>Sonoma   45,639   46,305  46,570<P>Jimmy    30,163   36,863  46,425<P>Cyclone   2,995        3       0<P>Typhoon       0    2,497   2,199<P><BR>In 91 the Cyclone accounted for 6.5% of total Sonoma sales and ended in 92 after a production total of 3 units.  One is in a museum, the 2nd was used in a crash test and the 3rd is owned by a GMC truck dealer in Michigan.  The Typhoon was a little more sucessful  with a build of 2,497 units in 92 accounting for 6.7% of sales but when the market rebounded in 93 with a 25 % sales increase of the Jimmy to 46,425 units the Typhoon sales were falling to 4.7%<P>Both of these trucks were engineering successes but not intended for the same in the sales arena especially when they were very expensive to build and had to be farmed outside the corporation.  By the way this is not an unusual practice as the Ttops in the Fiero, the Sunbird convertible and the 1st Ttops in the 76 Black Special Edition Trans Am were done by outside firms.  Why? GM did not have to make a big investment just pass the cost along to the consumer.<P>In the end the Cyclone & Typhoon were just examples of "Halo Effect" marketing.  A draw so to speak to get people to come into the dealer's showroom.<P>If Mr. Aldikacti has stated in an interview that the Chevy guys were always looking over their shoulder I'm sure he meant it but so many times the Automotive Press likes to throw some sensationalism into their stories.  If Hulki does have some sour grapes who could blame him?  Stop and think.  Would you rather be the father of the Corvette, America's symbol of performance muscle car in several generations or the father of the Fiero, one of GM's best ideas but greatest failures.<P>I think I would tend to blame a lot of people beside myself when I had put my heart and sole into it as he did.<P>I'm sick to this day when I think of what the corporation did by not putting forth the total effort on this carline until it was too late.  Remember Roger Smith was still in charge and he spent over 75 billion dollars to upgrade and modernize GM's assmebly plants with high tech robotics but ended up with over capacity to build cars the public did not want.  They closed 22 assembly plants soon after.  Again too little too late now instead of the 65 % market share GM had in the 60's they are squeaking by with a 30 something % share.<P>This has gone on longer than I had wanted this to but these are the cold hard facts.<P>Just be glad to own a piece of automotive history if you are lucky enough to own a Fiero.  They will only increase in value as time goes by.<P>Mike Murphy<BR>District Sales Manager <BR>Pontiac Motor Division<BR>1982-1987||||reg||3
Z||000001||Dave Gunsul||11-22-2001||12:18 AM||||This has only gone on to long because you keep it going by spewing the same crap<BR>that we've all heard time and time again<BR>from the news media. <BR>This post is obviously flame bait so i'm not going to bite this time by throwing insults<BR>about your lack of brain matter and common sense right back at you....oops, i guess i just did.<BR>I do agree with some of your facts but<BR>i'm very tired of this battle and waiting for another of your long winded, eds type postings, to come back every week.<BR>Also, as I said before, this is a pointless argument anyway because we aren't going to be able to convince each other to change each others minds.<BR>Lets just agree to disagree and everyone can believe what they want. I personally will still believe Hulki especially because<BR>he certainly isn't bitter about anything.<P>Now go a head and get the last word in Mike<BR>'cause I know you have to. <BR>I have the feeling you're used to that.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver<p>[This message has been edited by Dave Gunsul (edited 11-22-2001).]||||reg||1
