Z||000013||Mike Murphy||11-23-2001||01:41 PM||||Dave: I've never disputed the fact that Chevy was not against the car from the begining.  The top executives that set on the planning committee did have input from Chevrolet in the begining and 2 seaters in general were not a viable market the corporation really wanted.  It took a lot of convincing on Pontiac's behalf to win approval.  Unfortunately with only a 300 million budget which is a shoestring but that's why the car was not up to speed.<P>All I've matained is that the lack of sales is what killed it in the end.  The Washington Post states it was only profitable 2 out of 5 years.<P>GM has never wanted to supplement the profits from one car line to another just to maintain production.<P>Remember the Buick 2 seat Reatta?  It was a good design even with off the self parts but in a personal luxury class.  It's sales killed it too.<P>The engineers & sales people do have good ideas but the financial side has more influence thus making executive committee decisions too conservative when it comes time to build the car that was originaly proposed. <P>GM wants high volume bread & butter vehicles were they can make the most profit but if anyone can screw up a good idea I think they are tops.<P>I was so fed up with the goings on within GM that I walked away from a great position because of all the internal turmoil on the reorganization of GM.  <P>Murph||||reg||12
Z||000014||Eriamjh||11-24-2001||01:10 PM||||Funny.  Neither of these posts mention the horrible fire defect of the '84s causing 2% of them to be burned to a crisp.<P>Or the fact that the Sagnaw foundry that produced the connecting rods produced 1 to 4 defective (cracked) rods for every 10 made and REFUSED to improve their quality.<P>Or that the engine blocks had a casting defect that contributed to oil loss resulting in the fires.<P>Or that the heads also had a casting defect resulting in oil loss.<P>Or that the Fiero engine was a carryover engine with a shallower oil pan that ran on 3 quarts of oil instead of 4, making oil loss a more serious problem.<P>Or that GM blamed the Fiero fires on "owner neglegence".<P>Or that Fiero fires and GMs lack of a response in the form of a recall of repair caused customers to stay away from the car helping contribute to lower sales each year.<P>Or that every single Fiero ever made was eventually recalled for one defect or another.<P>It was a mircale that the car ever got produced.  GM took a lot of off the shelf parts, spent a little in custom tooling for the space-frame and made a beatiful econo-box with decent styling and decent handling.  It handfed like neither a chevette or a citation, but it had their suspensions.<P>GM made every effort not to support the Fiero once it went into production.  By 1988, Fieros were $15000, not the $7999 they started at in the fall of '83.  GM jacked up the price to counter falling sales and sales fell even farther.  Coupled with "fire" problems and a bad consumer view of the car, the damage was done by 1988.<P>Now GM is quick to discontinue parts and ignore the car further.  Will the Fiero ever come back?  Not in name, but possibly in design.  There has not been a mid-engined two-seater produced in the US since and may never be.  GM blamed the market for the failure of the Fiero, but in 1989, the Miata came aboard and suddenly two-seaters were hot again.  <P>The Fiero could have been a Miata.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000015||mrfiero||11-24-2001||02:31 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Eriamjh:<BR><B>By 1988, Fieros were $15000, not the $7999 they started at in the fall of '83.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I agree with all of your points except this one.  You are comparing apples and oranges here.  The base price of an '84 econo-coupe was indeed $7999, but the only $15,000 '88 Fieros were loaded GT's......the base model '88 coupe was $8999, so the entry level car only rose $1000.  <P><BR>I just did some calculating and in all actuality, the base model '84 Fiero was more expensive than the base model '88 Fiero.<P>A 1983 dollar was only worth 84 cents in 1988, so that $7999 price in 1983 was equivelant to $9500 in 1988 ($7999/.84)......$500 more than the base price in 1988.  So GM should have raised the price another $500 in 1988 to keep up with inflation!<P>That said.....there were plenty of 1984 SE's going for $12-$13K (sometimes higher than this!) when the demand was high, so that factors to about $14-$15K in 1988 dollars......about the same as a loaded '88 GT.<P>Interesting stuff.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by mrfiero (edited 11-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||gtjeff||11-24-2001||02:48 PM||||1988 GT's were usually found with sticker prices of around 16,400-16,600 with automatic (base price that year for GT was around 13,999). A fully loaded GT with t-tops had a sticker of around 19,999. <BR>Amazing but today 16,400 is what some Cavaliers sticker for.||||reg||1
Z||000017||mrfiero||11-24-2001||02:53 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by gtjeff:<BR><B>1988 GT's were usually found with sticker prices of around 16,400-16,600 with automatic (base price that year for GT was around 13,999). A fully loaded GT with t-tops had a sticker of around 19,999. <BR>Amazing but today 16,400 is what some Cavaliers sticker for.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>The base price of an '88 GT was $13,999, but no '88 GT ever had a sticker price of $20K.  The most bloated sticker price I ever saw was for $18,400 on a fully loaded (leather, T-Tops, etc.) '88 GT.  <P>A car that was $16,400 in 1988 would be equivelant to a $24,600 car in 2001 (a 1988 dollar equals $1.50 in 2001).  That means a $16,400 car in 2001 would have been like buying a $10,800 car in 1988 (the 2001 dollar equals 66 cents in 1988). <BR><p>[This message has been edited by mrfiero (edited 11-24-2001).]||||reg||1
