Z||000024||ThatRickGuy||11-26-2001||04:26 AM||||hey, I couldn't sleep so I came to the forum  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> it's been a while but the ol' beast is still runnin. Anyways, I just wanted to tack on 2 things:<P>Rise in cost: it was all about the insurance. My mother hwas an insurance sales person during this time period. it seems that the fiero was a very high theft rate car, and as such comprehensive insurance on it kept going up. not because of the fires or bad drivers or anything. Another factor on the insurance is the design of the car. the #2 savest car all years it was produced. Unfortunatly, it would save the passanger at the expense of the body, thus a 25mph hit which may be fixable on a solid frame or unibody car, would send a fiero to the salvage yard. To this day, there are some insurance companies that refuse to cover fiero's (I beleive Omni Insurance is one)<P>and my second thing, I personally don't see the fiero as that big of failure. The idea behind the fiero was bigger then the car, as some people mentioned, it was also a restructuring of the corperation ideal, with a new hands on, everyone is important feel to it, while yes the car line died, the new management style continued on, and the technology from the car lived with it in what we now call Saturns. So anytime you see a saturn cruise by, just think of it as your car's child.<P>anyway, that's my 3am need sleep rant. have fun!||||reg||1
Z||000025||Eriamjh||11-26-2001||08:19 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>I agree with all of your points except this one. You are comparing apples and oranges here. The base price of an '84 econo-coupe was indeed $7999, but the only $15,000 '88 Fieros were loaded GT's......the base model '88 coupe was $8999, so the entry level car only rose $1000.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yeah, you're right.  I don't have the base vs. fully loaded prices in front of me, but I also don't know what the mix of loaded versus base cars was.  The book I read which quoted the rising price of the car as one reason sales dipped.<P>What models sold the hottest?  Base or SE/GT (by percent each model year).<P>And, because of the fires, the insurance companies tended to jack up the rates for young drivers (who drove Fieros like idiots anyways) and being a new model in '84, they also upped collision rates even though the bolt on panels were supposed to be "cheap and easy to replace" according to GM.  I think I remember hearing people paying $2000 a year to insure their Fieros back then.<P>All I know is now I pay $320 a year for mine with no collision or comp.||||reg||1
Z||000026||Oreif||11-26-2001||09:05 AM||||As for GM missing the boat on the popularity of the Fiero,<P>I did a search,<BR>F-bodies: 261 websites.<BR>(I typed in all the names of the F-body cars and added them all up.)<BR>Corvette: 245 websites.<BR>Corvair: 492 websites.<BR>Fiero: 3600 websites!||||reg||1
Z||000027||TylerB11||11-26-2001||12:37 PM||||Murph - I think the reason that fiero owners love to believe the "Corvette Camp killed the fiero because they were afraid" rumor is obvious. Would you love to be the guy that owned a cheap car that put the fear of god into the very people who make that "american supercar"? Fiero owners WANT to believe it because we want to be able to snicker at every corvette we see, and laugh at its owner for paying so much for it.<P>I guess it all comes down to the usual competitive spirit of the car owner. <P>Come on people, lets get serious, the corvette group didn't kill the fiero. GM shot itself in the foot (and by the looks of the aztek, they haven't learned)||||reg||1
Z||000028||84Bill||11-26-2001||03:25 PM||||In the words of my hero "Dexter" That is just Stuuuupid, plain Stuuuupid! <P>GM created the Fiero on a shoestring budget, far less went into the development than any other car of it's time, One can only guess why. What GM ended up with was a totaly unique car design that if it had made it to adolescence would have been undoubtedly the Lamborgini of America. This dumpy under powered collection of mismatched parts that could not stop to save its own arse, was built on a (the real innovation) space frame covered with cheap plastic was never intended to be more than just that, cheap high profit transportation. If you have driven an 84 2.5 slushbox equipped Fiero you know what I mean. LETS face it, when problems loomed and suits abounded GM pulled the plug. It just makes good business sense to do so.<BR>Why in the world would any company throw expensive support FIX a product and lawyers to fight suits for ANY reason let alone another companies manufacturing flaws. Lets not forget that the fiero was not the 2.5 L4 to burst into flames, even to this day cars burn up from engine fires! but they still build them. SUV's roll over like attention starved puppies yet auto manufacturers make and sell them like crazy.<BR>But give ANY of them bad press for 3 years and see what happens. <BR>The Fiero was all bark and damn near ZERO bite in the performance department but if you wanted a new car really really cheap and you were single and wanted a really awesome cool sporty look the Fiero was the car for you. Bottom line.... cheap. Cheaper to build cheap to buy.  Oh and cheap connecting rods to boot.<P>Corporate mentality, economics and consumer mentality driven by the lack of support by GM killed the Fiero and maybe even the name FIREo didn't help. After all they did spontaneously combust. <BR>GM had "potentially" the stuff of legends in it's stable but money right NOW makes the world go round, not "maybe money" after we tweak it a bit here and there and perform expensive recalls along the way. <BR>You all know and I'm sure you all feel the same way I do for a car that was never designed to be nothing more than a "sporty looking" grocery getter (and a bad one even at that), it sure is FUN to drive! Fun may well sell cars in a booming market where people have cash to burn but reviews and statistics from Car & Driver can make a good car look really bad. Smart consumers read about things they will be paying for over the next 3 to 5 years to be sure they are getting the most bang for the buck.<P>Car companies have no vision beyond making money. What I mean by that is take Chrysler for example. They built a radical looking car (prowler) just for people who were looking for something with oldish looking style, with an open cockpit but new in design and materials. So with a few orders in hand they built the car. Now they have moved on with the PT Cruiser and it's POPULAR! and most people love the styling. Sheesh come on! 1930's fender flairs! It was a gamble and crystler is doing well with it. <P>Lets suppose the PT for some odd reason were to burst into flames from some design flaw in the engine or the Prowlers aluminum frame cracked over time and could lead to catastrophic failure at cruising speeds. How long do you suppose they could sustain bad press and law suits?<BR>Yeah, I though so.<BR>If that aint enough for you then I have a crapload of Firestone tires for sale. I got them cheap from the Ford dealer. Ok how about Stock in Firestone? Anyone... Anyone?<P>GM and other manufacturing companies want our money not our applause. What killed the Fiero was the Fiero itself. The car was not designed with full and total (very expensive) uniqueness.  Had it been I'm sure we would still see Fieros at our dealers and the first ones would have been undoubtedly double maybe triple the sticker price. By now the Fiero would most likely have been a sports car of legendary proportions as it was back in 84 right out of the box. Sadly the world will never know.<P>If it dont make money it aint worth making||||reg||1
Z||000029||terryk||11-26-2001||03:44 PM||||I have to say this. No, really, I *have* to. It's federal law. It's not just one of those state things.<P>The Fiero is not a Vette. I don't care how much money you throw at it in R&D, the Fiero will never be a Vette without becoming a Vette. If that happens, the Vette will just go even further and be even better. <P>Mid-engine? Ok, so let Vette pull the mid-engine prototypes out. Who wins? Vette. The only way a Fiero could beat a Vette is to become a Vette. Either way, Vette wins. There are Vette prototypes that were *Fiero's* before the Fiero project existed.<P>Apples to apples. Is there anyone that would race their stock Fiero against a stock C4 for titles? No? <P>Is there anyone that would race their modified Fiero against a modifed Vette for titles? No? <P>So, the only bets are a modified Fiero against a stock Vette? Yeah. And I'll bet the stock Vette will win then too. Now it's possible the Vette won't win......Wanna bet?<P>Vette had nothing to fear other than Pontiac getting into their marketing segment. It's corporate's job to keep that from happening. Allowing Fiero into that market would have been stupid. And that was very unlikely to happen. <P>I do believe the Vette team got torqued periodically when they were beat in show-off factor by a few of the cars and trucks (GN/TR's, SyTy, etc.). But marching into Pontiac and saying "stop production, we're a-scared"? If Fiero's were making money, GM would still be cranking them out. This is basic Madison Avenue Marketing 101. <P>But wait? How about Vette just putting the Fiero name on it? Would that make everyone feel different?<P>Oh, one more thing. I will take my Fiero over a Vette any day. One, I fit. Two, I don't look like I'm going through my mid-life crisis. Maybe that's why I am anti-Vette. Maybe that's why I prefer the Fiero and GN/TR's to the Vette. But let's give Vette it's due. It is one hell of a car in any form.<P>84Bill, I saw your reply above right after I posted. That says it all. Dead on. What GM needed was all of this enthusiasm 15 years ago and people with money. The rest is Monday morning quarterbacking.<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 11-26-2001).]||||reg||7
